# SSH Brute-Force Detection and Incident Investigation Lab

## Project Status
In Progress

## Overview
A practical cybersecurity home lab focused on detecting,
analysing and investigating suspicious SSH authentication
activity using Linux and Python.

## Objectives
- Configure an Ubuntu SSH server.
- Generate controlled authentication events from Kali Linux.
- Collect and examine authentication logs.
- Develop a Python-based log analysis tool.
- Identify suspicious authentication patterns.
- Document investigation findings and response recommendations.

## Lab Environment

| Machine | Operating System | IP Address | Role |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kali | Kali Linux ARM64 | 10.0.1.129 | Security testing |
| Ubuntu | Ubuntu Server ARM64 | 10.0.1.131 | Target server |
| Windows | Windows 10 ARM64 | 10.0.1.132 | Future endpoint monitoring |

Network: VMware Fusion NAT
Subnet: 10.0.1.0/24

## Planned Workflow
1. Verify network connectivity.
2. Verify SSH server configuration.
3. Generate controlled authentication events.
4. Collect authentication logs.
5. Analyse logs manually.
6. Develop Python detection logic.
7. Investigate detected activity.
8. Document findings and limitations.

## Repository Structure
- architecture/: Lab architecture and network diagrams.
- setup/: Environment configuration.
- scripts/: Python detection and analysis scripts.
- evidence/: Sanitised logs and screenshots.
- investigations/: Individual investigation cases.
- reports/: Final incident reports.
- docs/: Technical documentation and lessons learned.

## Safety
All testing is restricted to virtual machines owned
and controlled by the author within the private lab.

No external systems are targeted.

## Disclaimer
This repository documents a learning project.
All findings will be added only after practical verification.
